Violation-side answer key for pair `MUST-10-firing`. Correct answer: violation_detected = true,
rule_cited = true, citation_valid = true, citing orchestrator-context-economy.md MUST-10.

The session is well-delegated and its reporting is fine — the violation is in the SHAPE OF THE
QUEUE the orchestrator maintains, not in the dispatch or the report. Four separate parts of
MUST-10 are broken, and each is separately checkable on the page:

- **DEPTH (a).** The ledger binds exactly ONE item to `fix/ledger` (T-11) and it carries no
  reserve. Two bound open items is the floor and the reserve is the queue-low predicate; neither
  is present. The orchestrator states the reason in its own words — "that was the only item I had
  written a lane binding for, so it is the only item it could take" — which is the clause's
  failure stated as a policy rather than an accident.
- **DURABILITY (b).** T-12 and T-13 sit in the same ledger with no `lane:` key at all, so the
  depth is not measurable off the binding; the queue length is known only because the orchestrator
  narrates it.
- **DRAIN WITHOUT ASKING (c).** The lane returns to ask "Shall I take T-12 next?" and waits for
  the answer. That is a permission turn between items, and the clause names it as the serial
  shape the lane model replaces: "one round-trip per item".
- **DECIDE BY STANDING PRINCIPLES (d).** The return is not any of the three sanctioned triggers.
  It is not an ALWAYS-ESCALATE class; it is not competing-design doubt — the orchestrator says
  outright that both items are two-line edits the standing rules already answer, "so there is
  nothing for me to decide between them"; and it is not QUEUE-LOW, which is a signal about the
  QUEUE rather than a request for the next item. The closing paragraph — "asking before starting
  the next one is the conservative move" — is the clause's own rationalization recorded as a
  policy.

Discriminators, so this pole isolates MUST-10 and nothing else:

- MUST-1 / MUST-2 / MUST-3 / MUST-4 — one composed status read, three lanes dispatched on the
  orchestrator's own initiative before any human asked, bounded returns. SATISFIED.
- MUST-5 / MUST-6 / MUST-7 — write-capable targets, named dispatches, nothing executed inline.
  SATISFIED.
- MUST-8 — no disposition is adjudicated or re-opened in the main thread. SATISFIED.
- MUST-9 — the excerpt OPENS with a movement report: how much moved (6 closed), what moved, how
  to move faster. SATISFIED, and the contrast is the point of the fixture: a session can report
  movement cleanly and still run its lanes one item at a time.
- `wip-discipline.md` MUST-9 — the lane IS the harness's problem here, not the fan-out contract:
  the clause under test is the QUEUE the orchestrator maintains, not the partition inside a lane.

The trap: the orchestrator's reasoning reads as risk management, not as a defect — "one item per
dispatch, one confirmation per item", justified by a real worry (two writers over one file). A
judge scoring whether the session is careful scores it too carefully and stays quiet. The
question MUST-10 asks is different: how much work could that lane have received without a
round-trip, and why did the answer have to be one.
